    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9077A\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend subdivisions 1 and 9 of section 1 of chapter 693 of the\nlaws of 1980, enabling the county of Albany to impose and collect taxes\non occupancy of hotel or motel rooms in Albany county, in relation to\nthe amount of tax authorized; and to amend section 3 of chapter 105 of\nthe laws of 2009, amending chapter 693 of the laws of 1980 enabling the\ncounty of Albany to impose and collect taxes on occupancy of hotel or\nmotel rooms in Albany county relating to revenues received from the\ncollection of hotel or motel occupancy taxes, in relation to the effec-\ntiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nExtends the ability of the County of Albany to impose and collect taxes\non occupancy of hotel or motel rooms in Albany county and increases the\ntax by one-half percent of the per diem rental rate.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends subdivision 1 of section 1 of chapter 693 of the laws\nof 1980 to enable Albany County to collect a hotel or motel tax.\n\nSection two extends the occupancy tax to December 31, 2027 Section three\nestablishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill is at the request of Albany County and extends the ability of\nthe County of Albany to impose and collect taxes on occupancy of hotel\nor motel rooms in Albany County which is set to expire December 31,\n2024. The current six percent tax provides support for the Albany\nConvention Center Authority (3 percent), the Albany County Civic Center\n(2 percent) and the Albany County Convention and Visitors Bureau (1\npercent). This bill would continue that support and add an additional\none-half percent to the tax to provide operational support for the Coun-\nty's economic development organization, the Advance Albany County Alli-\nance Local Development Corporation (the Alliance).  The Alliance was\nformed in 2020 to promote economic development in Albany County through\nactivities that include but are not limited to:  (1) business retention,\nexpansion, and attraction; (2) development of shovel-ready sites; and\n(3) promotion of workforce development. The Alliance serves as a ones-\ntop-shop for economic development in the County, centralizing the Coun-\nty's economic development functions and improving coordination with\nmunicipal partners. Since its inception in 2020, the work of the Alli-\nance has led to increased business investment and job growth in the\nCounty, diversifying the County's tax base and enhancing quality of life\nfor its residents. The revenue generated by the proposed one-half point\nincrease will ensure the Alliance has sustainable funding to maintain\nand expand.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2023: A7009/S6687 - chapter 337\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to the state\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
